---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE } from "../site.ts";
---
<Base title="About" description="Who runs the registry, how it is funded, and how to reach us.">
  <article class="prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
    <h2>Who runs it</h2>
    <p>Because of AI is kept by one editor, <a href="https://davegoldblatt.com" rel="me author">Dave Goldblatt</a> (<a href={`https://x.com/${SITE.editorX}`} rel="me author">@{SITE.editorX}</a> on X), who writes the rules and does not review entries. No human reviews entries: a pipeline scans the literature daily, a model from one vendor drafts an entry from the primary source with a receipt for every number, and a model from a different vendor must independently reach the same conclusion from the same source, or the entry is held. Held entries and the disagreements behind them are <a href="/changelog#held">published</a>, and every verification is on the public record. The models grading an AI registry have an interest in the answer; cross-vendor checking and the receipts are how the registry handles that, not a claim to have removed it. The <a href="/method#pipeline">method page</a> says exactly what the code does. There is no editorial board.</p>
    <h2>Funding</h2>
    <p>Independent and unfunded. No company, foundation, or model vendor pays for this site or has a say in what it includes.</p>
    <h2>Conflicts</h2>
    <p>Every entry states the conflicts we found: who funded the study, whether the vendor ran it, whether an author works for the company. Where a number comes from a company's own dashboard, the entry says so.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Propose or dispute an entry through the <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new/choose`}>public repository</a>. New entries are posted on X as <a href={`https://x.com/${SITE.x}`} rel="me">@{SITE.x}</a>; the editor is <a href={`https://x.com/${SITE.editorX}`} rel="me">@{SITE.editorX}</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
